
           IPW -- Image Processing Workbench -- ERL-C Version


  This version of IPW is a merger of the original work done by Jim Frew at
UCSB, his subsequent work, and the work done at the EPA Environmental
Research Laboratory, Corvallis, Oregon.  

  IPW is available via anonymous ftp at morpheus.cor.epa.gov in
/pub/misc/ipw/ipw-dist.tar.gz.  Documentation and source are available
to government sites from the WWW server at
http://ice.cor.epa.gov/projects/ipw/.


  The header files and some of the programs have been updated to handle
ANSI C if it is available, which provides better error checking and
better portability.  Some of the base scripts and programs have been
changed in addition.

  The biggest change from the original offering is the use of GNU Make
instead of ipwmake.  While this requires porting GNU Make to each
machine, I don't believe this represents a significant difficulty, and the
benefits of using GNU Make extend beyond IPW.  The problem with ipwmake
is it's assumption of a Release 7 Make -- one with limited functionality.

  Why use GNU Make instead of assuming BSD Make?  By using GNU Make I
can guarantee what features are available.  Some people's makes are just
not the same as others.  Also, GNU Make has some features not available
in most makes, such as conditionals, simple variables, parallel makes,
functions for string substitutions, and more.

  What if I can't get GNU Make working?  I have left the Makedefs files
in directories where they existed.  At some point I will remove these,
but for now they are left.  I have made no effort to modify them however.



  Eventually, IPW will use an autoconfigure script to handle most parts
of the installation.  Currently it uses canned configuration scripts,
but there is a configure program that will install the correct versions
for you, unlike what Chapter 8 of the documentation says.


0) Install gnumake.  This should be available either where you got IPW,
   or via anonymous ftp at prep.ai.mit.edu:/pub/gnu/make-3.74.tar.z.  If
   you do not install gnumake as "make", replace the work "make" in all
   further instructions with the name you installed it as (ie "gnumake").
            >> NORMAL MAKE WILL NOT WORK WITH IPW. <<
   Read the above reasons for using GNU Make.  If this really annoys you,
   send me mail.  Note that any newer version should work just fine, and
   I know that version 3.62 and above also work.  Generally, if you have
   any version of GNU make from the last 5 years, you should be fine.

   The file INSTALL in the GNU make directory should contain all the
   information needed.  What follows is a step-by-step method that
   should be plug'n'chug.  For most sites, typing configure; make; make install
   will be adequate.

   a) Get make-3.74.tar.gz.  This is the newest version as of August 1995.
      It uses an auto-configuration script, so it should be straightforward
      to compile even on very odd systems.  Uncompress (or gunzip) the file,
      then untar it.  Change to it's directory.

   b) Where is it going to be installed?  The default prefix is "/usr/local"
      which means 'make' will be installed in /usr/local/bin, and the manual
      will go in /usr/local/man/man1/make.1.  To change this behaviour, add
      "--prefix=something" to the ./configure statement (see next step).  For
      example, to install 'make' in /usr/local/gnu/bin and the manual pages
      in /usr/local/gnu/man/man1/make.1, add "--prefix=/usr/local/gnu" to the
      ./configure statement.

   c) Run "./configure".  If you wanted it installed in a different
      directory, don't forget the "--prefix=..." part.  ./configure will
      test what functionality your operating system has, and should
      create a Makefile that has all the proper options in it.  This is
      much easier than trying to do all the work yourself.  If you want
      make in /usr/local/bin/make, type "./configure".  If you want make
      in your personal bin directory, type "./configure --prefix=$HOME".

      c1)  If you're on a Data General, you may have to change config.h
         from "#undef DIRENT" to "#define DIRENT 1".  I haven't checked
         to see how the newer make versions handle this.

   d) What will it be called?  By default, GNU make will be installed as
      'make'.  If the directory GNU make is installed in is before
      /usr/bin in your path, GNU make will be executed.  If /usr/bin is
      first in your path, you will not be using GNU make when you type 
      'make'.  A solution is to call GNU make something different.  Usual
      names are 'gnumake' or 'gmake'.  This lets you use the system make 
      as 'make' and GNU make as whatever the new name is.  To change the
      name that is used, after running configure (the previous step),
      edit the file "Makefile" and change the line "binprefix =" to set the
      desired prefix, such as "binprefix = gnu" to install as "gnumake".

   e) If your current system does not have a make program, the configure
      program will have made a Bourne shell script called "build.sh".  Type
      "sh build.sh" to compile GNU make.  If you already have a version of
      make, then just "make" should compile GNU make.

   f) Install GNU make.  "make install" should do it.

   g) If you are using csh or tcsh, type "rehash" so it will find the new
      make.
 




1) set the IPW variable.  If you are using the C shell, you can go to
   the top IPW directory (where this file is located) and type:
      setenv IPW `pwd`
   Those are backquotes surrounding the pwd.

2) configure for your machine type.  Examples: 'configure sun4',
   'configure ibm', 'configure 'hp730', 'configure sun3-sunos3'.  For a
   list of supported configurations, type 'configure -list'.  If configure
   complains about GNU make not being available, you _must_ fix that before
   going on.
   The arguments for configure are 1) a processor architecture, and 2) an
   operating system.  The operating system is optional since each architecture
   has a default operating system for it.  This may not be the one you are
   running, however.

3) configure for the local installation.

   3a) Change the scratch directory.  This should point at wherever you want
       temporary files to go.  You can do 'rmdir scratch; ln -s /tmp scratch',
       or you can link it to a larger disk.  Alternately, you can leave it as
       it is, which is as a world-writable directory on the same disk that
       the IPW directory is on.

   3b) Make sure the local Makefile options are correct.  Edit the file
       lib/make/local and make sure everything is correct for your
       installation.  This is where the default compiler gets set, the
       default optimization levels, what your archiver and ranlib commands
       are called, etc.  The values given are probably correct.

4) make the sources.  'make install'.  Optionally, you can go through
   the long way (if things aren't going quite right or you want to take
   it slow.  Either way, installation on a fast machine (Sparc 2 or HP 7xx
   machine) will take about an hour.  What follows is a list of commands
   you can use to install IPW in smaller chunks.  This is very similar to
   what just typing 'make install' does.

    4a) Make sure we can install: 'cd $IPW/src/etc/install; make install'
    4b) make the libraries: 'cd $IPW/src/lib; make'
    4c) install the libraries if they all compiled: 'make install'
    4d) install scripts: 'cd $IPW/src/etc; make install'
    4e) make standard ipw programs: 'cd $IPW/src/bin; make'
    4f) install them if all made ok: 'make install'
    4g) install contributed programs: 'cd $IPW/src/exp; make'
        (There will be some errors about mising libraries at the link stage.
         These will be taken care of at the next step)
    4h) 'make install'

   By default, if an error is encountered, make will stop at that point.  If
   you are porting IPW to a new system, this may be undesirable.  Use the
   '--keep-going' (abbreviation '-k') option of GNU make to continue past
   the error.  This lets you compile everything that is going to work, just
   leaving the programs that failed to deal with.

   A useful procedure if you are getting errors is to redirect the output
   of make to a file, then search for any errors after make is done.  This
   same technique can also be used when running the tests in the next step.

   You might also find that if you are using a new GNU make (3.65 or newer),
   its default behaviour of printing each directory name is annoying and can
   hinder finding errors.  If you use the argument '--no-print-directory' to
   GNU make, the output will be easier to read.

5) run tests.  'cd $IPW/tests; make'.  Errors are indicated by asterisks and
   a message.  Certain programs will not be tested because their behavior
   is either not reproducible without problems (ipw, binexp), or they
   read from tape (tmpt, dempt), or other reasons.  They will be indicated
   by the line "Not testing program" which is not to be construed as an error
   message.  Some programs may have the appendage "(sp)" added to the
   "Program passed" message.  This means one of two things:

     a) your program evaluates float expressions in float precision rather
        than casting to doubles first.  In standard K&R C, all floats were
        cast to doubles first, but ANSI C specified that arithmetic on float
        operands may be done in single precision.  This can make some
        expressions evaluate faster, but the result will be slightly
        different.  In some cases your machine will come up with a different
        result, and the program will return "Small float diff" which means
        the result is off by a small amount which is most likely caused by
        small differences in floating point evaluation.

     b) Your system is missing a function such as random(), and a
        functionally equivalent function has been substituted.  The new
        function may return different values for the same input, if that
        is allowed by the function definition (e.g. random).

6) after everthing is successfuly installed, you can reduce disk space
   usage by: 'make clean' which will remove all the .o files left around.

Notes:

-----
       If you wish to clean up everything, including the compiled binaries
     and libraries, the command 'make realclean' is available.  The test
     directory can also be removed to free up more space.

-----
       If a program requires an ANSI C compiler, the IPW variable ANSI_CC
     is defined in $IPW/lib/make/local.  In the programs Makefile, put
     'CC=$(ANSI_CC)' somewhere.  None of the programs supplied here require
     an ANSI C compiler, and it is recommended that you use the ansidecl
     macros to make your programs compliant with both K&R and ANSI C.
       The following defaults are made for ANSI_CC:

     HP/UX: Currently this is "cc -Aa" which turns on ANSI support for HP's
            C Compiler.  Not all Machine/OS combinations support this, so
            you may have to get gcc to support ANSI.  gcc happens to produce
            better code than HP's bundled C compiler in most cases anyway.
     SunOS: Currently this is "gcc" which supports ANSI C.  If you have the
            Sun Unbundled C Compiler, you can change this to "acc" which is
            Sun's ANSI C Compiler.  Using gcc with the "-Wall" option gives
            better debugging than acc with "-vc".

       gcc is available from prep.ai.mit.edu:/pub/gnu/gcc-2.7.0.tar.gz.
     (It is possible that there will be a newer version there).  Gcc is
     highly recommended in any circumstance, as it solves many portability
     problems and often generates faster code.
       Some of the programs have shown problems with very old versions of GCC.
     By typing "gcc -v" you can see what version of gcc you are running.
     If your version of gcc is below 2.0, you should get the newer compiler.
     The problems stem from the optimizer -- systems which use GCC as their
     native compiler, Data General for instance, will supply you with a
     more recent version if you ask (DG ships version 1.96 with their 5.4.1
     systems, but they acknowledge the problems and will ship a 2.2.2 tape
     on request).

-----
     grhist uses the "graph" program.  Some sytems like DG/UX don't include
   it.  A version of graph along with a number of other graphics related
   programs (plot2ps and xplot are useful) is available from the GNU group
   as graphics-0.17.tar.gz.  Unfortunately, the GNU version of graph is
   slightly different from the BSD version.

-----
     To make the distributed tar file, assuming the name of the directory
   holding ipw is called "ipw", the following line is used:

        gnutar -cX ipw/exclude.list -zf ipw-dist.tar.gz ipw

     The following should also work, but exclude will have to be changed
   every time a new binary is added.

        tar -cFFXf ipw/exclude ipw-dist.tar ipw



